        "id": 1136059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-16T20:59:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Once we have a failure there\u0027s no way to recover other than invalidating nvmem? \n\nThere\u0027s no possible way for nvmem to succeed the next time? Is it possible we encountered an error for some flaky reason?\n\nCr50 rolling reboots happen pretty quickly. If we wait for a couple of resets it won\u0027t add that much time and this shouldn\u0027t happen very often.",
      "parentUuid": "4c15a13b_b2a5220e",
      "revId": "3d62e6019dd8b7a84ce380858869b06dd7e3ef11",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7de5c1fa_6affc033",
        "filename": "common/new_nvmem.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 381,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-08-16T21:13:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "it would be extra code for no good reason. Unless we think that flash reads happen to be wrong sometimes (and in this case the entire device would be falling apart in mysterious ways each time this happens, and this is not happening), so there is no such thing as \u0027flaky\u0027 nvmem failure.\n\nAlso notice that if the problem happened not within 10 seconds of reset it will still cause a reboot, so if the problem was due to a \u0027flake\u0027 it would not be seen again after reset.",
      "parentUuid": "48061d7a_1b58ebd2",
      "revId": "3d62e6019dd8b7a84ce380858869b06dd7e3ef11",
